  "preamble": "A velar nasal coda is lost and leaves its nasality on a close front vowel, so a Middle Chinese rhyme in -eŋ, -jeŋ or -æŋ ends up as [ĩ]. In Southern Min (Hokkien), these words have a vernacular reading in -iⁿ [ĩ] in Xiamen and Quanzhou, beside a literary reading that keeps the coda (-eng [iɪŋ]). Zhangzhou and much of southern Taiwan have -eⁿ [ẽ]/[ɛ̃] in the same words.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Nasalization, Coda Loss, Vowel Fronting",
